README.content:
# lib-ecs

An archetype ECS for Haxe games that need predictable memory, explicit simulation order, and rollback.

`lib-ecs` compiles your data schema and systems into a typed API for a particular game world. The same simulation can run forward, replay corrected input, and produce confirmed outputs for rendering, audio, or networking. You define the rules and budgets; the library manages their execution and state.

The primary target is **HashLink/C**. The core has no game-engine dependency and can sit beneath Heaps or another application layer.

## Why use it

| Capability | What it gives your game |
| --- | --- |
| Compile-time schema and access checks | Invalid component references, incompatible queries, and undeclared system rights fail during compilation. |
| Typed columns and generated access | Systems work with the data they declared, without string-based component lookup in their inner loops. |
| Explicit capacities | Entity storage, input history, and output buffers have planned limits. Exhaustion is a result to handle, rather than an unexpected growth policy. |
| One simulation timeline | Normal execution, prediction, late-input replay, and state correction share the same game rules. |
| Explicit publication boundaries | A write becomes visible at a chosen `Horizon`; execution order is part of the plan. |
| Typed external outputs | Rendering, sound, and other application objects stay outside the state that must be rolled back. |

This is useful when the simulation must remain understandable under load: which data a system can change, when another system observes it, and how much history a match retains are visible design decisions.

## Games it fits

The following are design fits, not claims of shipped games using the library.

| Genre or game structure | Why it fits |
| --- | --- |
| Fighting games, arena action, competitive platformers | Tick-based input, prediction, correction, and confirmed effects support a rollback-based simulation. The game still supplies its network protocol. |
| Tactical games, turn-based strategy, roguelikes | Ordered systems and restorable state help with replay, verification, and repeatable game rules. |
| Bullet hell, arena survival, tower defence | Many compact numerical entities and planned wave limits suit column storage and explicit capacities. |
| Match-based RTS, management games, bounded simulations | Resources, ownership graphs, and predictable execution suit interacting units and production rules when world limits are known. |
| Action RPGs and cooperative games | Prefabs describe related entities; input history and typed outputs keep gameplay separate from presentation. |

The deciding factors are the shape of the simulation and the need for replay or controlled memory, rather than the genre alone.

