## Build and run

This is the **1.0.0-rc.1** candidate. No haxelib release has been published. Start with a checkout you can access or an extracted source package. The runtime has no haxelib dependencies; Haxe, HashLink, and a native C toolchain are required for HL/C.

The checked toolchain is Haxe development `ccf17df` and HashLink master `864721a` (2.0.0). Compatibility with other compiler versions must be checked; “Haxe” alone is not a sufficient version specification.

For your application, use one library source directory and prepare its Plan before generated types appear in method signatures:

```hxml
-cp /path/to/lib-ecs/src
-cp src
-D ecs_weid32
-D hl-ver=2.0.0
--macro ecs.Blueprint.prepare("demo.GamePlan")
-main demo.Main
-dce full
-D no-compilation
-hl .build/main.c
```

Create `.build` before compilation. Select exactly one of `ecs_weid32` and `ecs_weid64`. Set `hl-ver` to the version in the `hl.h` headers used by the native compiler. The `.c` output selects HL/C; `no-compilation` leaves the native build to your build system.

To run all examples from the library root:

```sh
mkdir -p .build/examples
haxe examples/hlc.hxml -D ecs_weid32
```

On the tested macOS setup, `HASHLINK` points to an installation with `include/` and `lib/`:

```sh
cc -O2 -std=c11 -I .build/examples -I "$HASHLINK/include" \
  .build/examples/main.c -L "$HASHLINK/lib" \
  -Wl,-rpath,"$HASHLINK/lib" -lhl -o .build/examples/examples
.build/examples/examples
```

Expected output:

```text
ecs movement example ok
ecs prefab example ok
ecs pairs example ok
ecs rollback example ok
ecs snapshot example ok
ecs presentation example ok
```

The [example HXML](examples/hlc.hxml) compiles all six scenarios together. A [separate HXML](examples/movement/hlc.hxml) builds only the first example. Target-specific linking and SDK integration belong to the application's build.

| Example | What it demonstrates | Checked result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Movement](examples/movement/demo/Main.hx) | Schema, system, input, tick, confirmed effect | `x = 1.25` |
| [Prefab](examples/prefab/demo/PrefabMain.hx) | Create an actor and owned weapon in an empty world | Root health `90` |
| [Pairs](examples/pairs/demo/PairsMain.hx) | Visit each unordered pair once | Positions `0.75` and `1.75` |
| [Rollback](examples/timeline/demo/RollbackMain.hx) | Replace an unverified input revision and replay | Corrected `x = 1.25` |
| [Snapshot](examples/timeline/demo/SnapshotMain.hx) | Load a checkpoint in another Service and continue | Equal hash; continued `x = 2.0` |
| [Presentation](examples/presentation/demo/ViewMain.hx) | Fixed-step clock, shared resource, predicted output | `x = 0.5`; elapsed time `0.25` |

Package files are prepared locally. The tested haxelib client currently rejects `MPL-2.0` in its license field; [packaging notes](PACKAGING.md) record this publication blocker. Source-path use does not depend on haxelib accepting a submission.

## A complete first simulation

This example moves one actor. Its base speed is `2`, input adds `3`, and one tick has `dt = 0.25`, so the result is `(2 + 3) × 0.25 = 1.25`. The small capacities and four-tick frequency keep the example easy to inspect; they are game choices, not library limits.

### 1. Describe the data

`Fragment` describes one flat typed value. `Component` groups values at compile time. `Archetype` fixes which columns a row owns. A `Query` identifies the published rows a system should process.

Create [GameSchema.hx](examples/movement/demo/GameSchema.hx):

<!-- source: examples/movement/demo/GameSchema.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Blueprint;

@:build(ecs.Blueprint.definitions())
final class GameSchema {
  public static final position = Blueprint.component({x: Real(0.0)});
  public static final real_speed = Blueprint.fragment(Real, 2.0);
  public static final flag_alive = Blueprint.fragment(Flag, true);
  public static final actor = Blueprint.archetype([position, real_speed, flag_alive]);
  public static final living = Blueprint.query({equal: [flag_alive == true]});
}
```

The component field `position.x` becomes the typed fragment name `real_position_x`. `real_speed` and `flag_alive` are declared directly. These declarations describe storage; they do not allocate an actor for each declaration.

### 2. Define the simulation

An `Area` provides four actor slots. `bind` acquires the system's declared access once and returns its tick handler. The movement handler reads speed and input, then queues position changes. `AfterSystem` publishes those changes before the following observer runs.

Create [GamePlan.hx](examples/movement/demo/GamePlan.hx):

<!-- source: examples/movement/demo/GamePlan.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Blueprint;
import ecs.Horizon;

private enum abstract GameSystem(Int) {
  var Move = 0;
  var Observe = 1;
}

private enum abstract GameProcess(Int) {
  var Simulation = 0;
}

@:build(ecs.Blueprint.plan())
final class GamePlan {
  static final main = Blueprint.area([GameSchema.actor(4)]);
  static final position = Blueprint.effect({x: Float});
  static final move = Blueprint.system(main, GameSystem.Move, {
    objectives: [GameSchema.actor],
    query: GameSchema.living,
    bind: access -> {
      final speed = access.read.real_speed;
      final input = access.input;
      final set_x = access.set.real_position_x(Horizon.AfterSystem);
      return dt -> {
        final extra_speed = input.read_int32(0, 0);
        access.foreach(row -> {
          set_x.add(row, (speed[row] + extra_speed) * dt);
        });
      };
    },
  });
  static final observe = Blueprint.system(main, GameSystem.Observe, {
    objectives: [GameSchema.actor],
    query: GameSchema.living,
    bind: access -> {
      final x = access.read.real_position_x;
      final emit = access.effect(position);
      return _dt -> {
        access.foreach(row -> {
          emit(x[row]);
        });
      };
    },
  });
  static final simulation = Blueprint.process(GameProcess.Simulation, 1, process -> {
    process.add(move);
    process.add(observe);
  });
  static final plan = Blueprint.plan({
    areas: [main],
    processes: [simulation],
    input_capacity: 1,
    rollback_window: 4,
    effect_capacity: 4,
    time: Blueprint.fixed(4, 0.25),
  });
}
```

The process explicitly places movement before observation. The effect contains the resulting position and becomes available for final delivery after verification. The demo uses an effect to check its result; a continuously rendered scene normally reads a state output, shown below.

`Blueprint.fixed(4, 0.25)` sets **two independent values**: four ticks per second of application scheduling and a simulation step of `0.25`. The library does not infer one from the other.

### 3. Create a world and run one tick

`Service → Scope → World → Session` separates logical identity, the physical world's lifetime, stored state, and the simulation timeline. Before Session takes ownership, the application can populate initial entities with generated `spawn_…` methods.

Create [Main.hx](examples/movement/demo/Main.hx):

<!-- source: examples/movement/demo/Main.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Outcome;
import ecs.Service;
import ecs.Status;
import ecs.Tick;

final class Main {
  private static function accepted<T>(result: Outcome<T>): T {
    return switch result {
      case Accepted(value): value;
      case Rejected(status): throw new haxe.Exception('ECS: $status');
    };
  }

  private static function checked(status: Status): Void {
    if (status != Status.Ok) {
      throw new haxe.Exception('ECS: $status');
    }
  }

  public static function main(): Void {
    final service = new Service();
    final scope = accepted(GamePlan.open_scope(service));
    final world = accepted(scope.create_world(GamePlan.default_resource_init()));
    checked(world.spawn_actor());
    final session = accepted(GamePlan.session(world));
    final frame = GamePlan.input_frame();
    final batch = GamePlan.input_batch();
    final reader = GamePlan.new_effect_reader();
    final position = GamePlan.new_position_effect();
    final zero = Tick.zero();
    final one = accepted(Tick.at(0, 1));

    checked(frame.write_int32(0, 3));
    checked(batch.reset(zero));
    checked(batch.add(
      0,
      0,
      1,
      frame
    ));
    checked(session.submit(batch));
    checked(session.advance(one));
    checked(session.verify(one));
    checked(session.peekEffects(reader));
    checked(reader.read_position(position));
    if (position.x != 1.25) {
      throw new haxe.Exception('Incorrect position: ${position.x}');
    }
    checked(session.ackEffectsThrough(reader.key));
    checked(session.close());
    Sys.println('ecs movement example ok');
  }
}
```

`advance(one)` executes tick zero and reaches boundary one. `verify(one)` confirms the available input through that boundary. A successful effect delivery is acknowledged with its key, so the next read can advance. `session.close()` also closes its current Scope.

The example deliberately throws on unexpected results. A game should handle each returned `Status` or `Outcome` according to the operation: wait for input, retry after a reader is released, reject a command, or terminate a failed session. Avoid continuing dependent work after an unsuccessful result.

Allocate frames, batches, readers, records, and snapshot buffers when connecting a world and reuse them. Do not allocate a new copy for every visual frame merely because an example declares them inside `main()`.

## Data, queries, and publication order

### Choose the stored values

| Fragment kind | Intended value | Row storage |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Full` | Signed integer | Int32 |
| `Real` | Number in the Plan's numerical profile | Local Float64; Q48.16 under StrictFixed |
| `Flag` | Boolean | Packed bit |
| `Mask` | Bit mask | Int32 |
| `Byte` | Saturated value from 0 to 255 | Packed 8 bits |
| `Sign` | Normalised −1, 0, or +1 | Packed 2 bits |
| `enum abstract(Int)` | Application enum value | Int32 |
| `WEID`, `Host`, `Node` | Typed physical reference | Selected WEID profile |

Strings, arbitrary objects, `Dynamic`, and application collections are not component columns. Keep textures, scene nodes, audio handles, sockets, and asset descriptions outside the world; store the compact values your simulation actually needs.

Local `Real` rejects NaN and infinity at authoritative writes and canonicalises negative zero. StrictFixed is a separate numerical contract with checked Q48.16 arithmetic. Choosing it does not make an external floating-point physics engine deterministic.

### Select data and bind access

The first example's `equal: [flag_alive == true]` query excludes published dead actors. The prefab example uses `all: []` to visit every row of its declared objective. A Query does not invent the objective list: the system also declares which archetypes it processes.

Read a typed column through `access.read`, bind a deferred writer through `access.set`, and iterate the selected rows through `access.foreach`. Binding a name that the query and objective cannot provide is a compile-time error. A row is a temporary location for that traversal, not a persistent entity identity.

### Decide when writes become visible

| Horizon | Publication boundary |
| --- | --- |
| `AfterEntity` | After the current Entity |
| `AfterSystem` | After the current System |
| `AfterArchetype` | After the current Archetype |
| `AfterProcess` | After the current Process |
| `NextTick` | At the beginning of the next tick |

These boundaries are part of the algorithm. In the movement example, changing the setter to `NextTick` would change what the same-tick observer sees. A setter request does not immediately change the column that the current handler reads.

`Process` defines the overall order. `Flow` can organise a chain of subsystems for each objective. Feature declarations control optional parts of a Plan. These mechanisms organise known, compiled rules; they do not provide runtime registration of unknown component types.

## Prefabs and ownership

A prefab creates a fixed tree of related nodes as one structural operation. This is useful for a character with equipment, a projectile with an owned trail state, or a unit composed of several simulation nodes. Ownership belongs to ECS data; it does not have to mirror your scene graph.

Declare both sides of ownership in [PrefabSchema.hx](examples/prefab/demo/PrefabSchema.hx):

<!-- source: examples/prefab/demo/PrefabSchema.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Blueprint;

@:build(ecs.Blueprint.definitions())
final class PrefabSchema {
  public static final full_health = Blueprint.fragment(Full, 100);
  public static final full_damage = Blueprint.fragment(Full, 10);
  public static final node_weapon = Blueprint.fragment(Node(id.Archetype.weapon), 0);
  public static final host_actor = Blueprint.fragment(Host(id.Archetype.actor), 0);
  public static final actor = Blueprint.archetype([full_health, node_weapon]);
  public static final weapon = Blueprint.archetype([full_damage, host_actor]);
  public static final every = Blueprint.query({all: []});
}
```

`Node` is the forward ownership reference and `Host` is the reverse reference. Their initial zero means unset. The prefab supplies typed `ref` links; the application does not pack identifiers into these fields itself.

[PrefabPlan.hx](examples/prefab/demo/PrefabPlan.hx) declares the topology, permitted roots, capacity, and creation command:

<!-- source: examples/prefab/demo/PrefabPlan.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Blueprint;
import ecs.Horizon;

private enum abstract PrefabSystem(Int) {
  var Create = 0;
  var Observe = 1;
}

private enum abstract PrefabProcess(Int) {
  var Simulation = 0;
}

@:build(ecs.Blueprint.plan())
final class PrefabPlan {
  static final actors = Blueprint.group([PrefabSchema.actor]);
  static final population = Blueprint.part(id.Area.Main, {
    actor: PrefabSchema.actor(0),
    weapon: PrefabSchema.weapon(0),
    actors: [2, 2],
  });
  static final main = Blueprint.area(id.Area.Main, [population]);
  static final equipped = Blueprint.prefab({
    root: PrefabSchema.actor({full_health: leaf.health(80), node_weapon: ref.weapon}),
    weapon: PrefabSchema.weapon({host_actor: ref.root}),
  });
  static final health = Blueprint.effect({value: Int});
  static final create = Blueprint.system(main, PrefabSystem.Create, {
    objectives: [PrefabSchema.actor],
    query: PrefabSchema.every,
    bind: access -> {
      final input = access.input;
      final spawn_equipped = access.spawn.prefab(actors, equipped, Horizon.AfterSystem);
      return _dt -> {
        if (input.read_int32(0, 0) == 1) {
          spawn_equipped({health: 90});
        }
      };
    },
  });
  static final observe = Blueprint.system(main, PrefabSystem.Observe, {
    objectives: [PrefabSchema.actor],
    query: PrefabSchema.every,
    bind: access -> {
      final health = access.read.full_health;
      final emit = access.effect(health);
      return _dt -> {
        access.foreach(row -> {
          emit(health[row]);
        });
      };
    },
  });
  static final simulation = Blueprint.process(PrefabProcess.Simulation, 1, process -> {
    process.add(create);
    process.add(observe);
  });
  static final plan = Blueprint.plan({
    areas: [main],
    processes: [simulation],
    input_capacity: 1,
    rollback_window: 2,
    effect_capacity: 2,
    time: Blueprint.fixed(1, 1.0),
  });
}
```

`leaf.health(80)` exposes one parameter with a default. The creation request overrides it with `90`; the weapon keeps its default damage `10`. Ordinary internal fields remain part of the prefab definition. Presets can apply named value adjustments to declared compatible prefabs without redefining the tree.

The group admits actor roots and guarantees two complete trees, with no additional shared overflow in this example. `actor(0)` and `weapon(0)` give no direct standalone capacity; the group's prefab requirements supply their storage. A group limit counts roots created through that group, rather than every node in the tree as an unrelated entity.

The creation handler sits outside `foreach`, so it also runs when the world is empty. One input command `1` issues one request. Sending it again requests another tree. In a real game, decide whether an input value means a held action or a one-time command, and handle structural refusals when capacity is exhausted.

The [application entry point](examples/prefab/demo/PrefabMain.hx) starts with an empty world, submits `1`, advances and verifies the tick, and checks the health effect. It does not call `world.spawn_actor()` first. Setup spawning is unavailable once Session owns the world; runtime creation belongs inside declared system access.

## Pair interactions

For small sets, a direct pair system can express separation, short-range interactions, or a simple all-pairs rule without maintaining a second entity store. This example separates two coincident actors after movement:

<!-- source: examples/pairs/demo/PairPlan.hx -->
```haxe
static final separate = Blueprint.system(main, PairSystem.Pairs, {
  pairs: Blueprint.unique({
    actors: Blueprint.select(GameSchema.actor, GameSchema.living),
  }),
  bind: access -> {
    final x = access.actors.read.real_position_x;
    final set_x = access.actors.set.real_position_x(Horizon.AfterSystem);
    return _dt -> {
      access.pairwise((a, b) -> {
        final distance = x[b] - x[a];
        if (distance > -1.0 && distance < 1.0) {
          final shift = distance < 0.0 ? -0.5 : 0.5;
          set_x.sub(a, shift);
          set_x.add(b, shift);
        }
      });
    };
  },
});
```

The full [PairPlan](examples/pairs/demo/PairPlan.hx) inserts this system between movement and observation. [PairsMain](examples/pairs/demo/PairsMain.hx) creates two actors and checks the resulting positions `0.75` and `1.75`.

`Blueprint.unique` visits unordered pairs once (`i < j`). `Blueprint.cross` defines two named roles, each with its own selection and access, and excludes a row paired with its own WEID. Use cross-role pairs for interactions such as projectile versus target, where the roles have different meaning.

A pair system does not build a spatial index. All-pairs work grows quadratically. For a large world, use an application-owned broad phase, navigation algorithm, or physics integration through a `Procedure` and its bounded typed `feed`; the Plan keeps its execution barrier and the consuming systems' rights explicit.

## Rendering and shared resources

The simulation stores authoritative data; the application owns presentation objects. A `state` output publishes a typed view of the world, so rendering does not need private arrays, row addresses, or a reference to a mutable World.

This [ViewPlan](examples/presentation/demo/ViewPlan.hx) also adds a match clock as a Resource. A Resource is world state shared across systems, rather than a separate value for every actor. It participates in snapshots and hashes and is restored during replay.

<!-- source: examples/presentation/demo/ViewPlan.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Blueprint;
import ecs.Horizon;
import ecs.observe.ObservationCorrection;
import ecs.observe.ObservationSource;

private enum abstract ViewSystem(Int) {
  var Move = 0;
  var Present = 1;
}

private enum abstract ViewProcess(Int) {
  var Simulation = 0;
}

/** Publishes predicted render data and includes a match clock in rollback state. */
@:build(ecs.Blueprint.plan())
final class ViewPlan {
  static final main = Blueprint.area([GameSchema.actor(4)]);

  static final match = Blueprint.resources({
    elapsed: Blueprint.cell(Real, 0.0),
  });

  static final render = Blueprint.state({
    capacity: 4,
    source: ObservationSource.Predicted,
    correction: ObservationCorrection.Correct,
    overlap: 1,
    fields: {x: Float, elapsed: Float},
  });

  static final move = Blueprint.system(main, ViewSystem.Move, {
    objectives: [GameSchema.actor],
    query: GameSchema.living,
    bind: access -> {
      final speed = access.read.real_speed;
      final set_x = access.set.real_position_x(Horizon.AfterSystem);
      final clock = access.resource.write(match.elapsed);
      return dt -> {
        clock.add(dt);
        access.foreach(row -> set_x.add(row, speed[row] * dt));
      };
    },
  });

  static final present = Blueprint.system(main, ViewSystem.Present, {
    objectives: [GameSchema.actor],
    query: GameSchema.living,
    bind: access -> {
      final x = access.read.real_position_x;
      final elapsed = access.resource.read(match.elapsed);
      final publish = access.write(render);
      return _dt -> {
        access.foreach(row -> publish(row, x[row], elapsed.get()));
      };
    },
  });

  static final simulation = Blueprint.process(ViewProcess.Simulation, 1, process -> {
    process.add(move);
    process.add(present);
  });

  static final plan = Blueprint.plan({
    areas: [main],
    processes: [simulation],
    rollback_window: 4,
    time: Blueprint.fixed(4, 0.25),
  });
}
```

`Blueprint.cell` creates a scalar; `Blueprint.cells` creates a fixed-size collection. Initial values come from the Plan's `ResourceInit` when creating a world. Different initial values do not change the schema identity.

The movement system adds `dt` to the clock once per tick, outside the actor loop. The observer emits flat values after movement. Its `Predicted` source is suitable for responsive presentation; `Correct` specifies correction behaviour when replay changes that output.

The application creates and reuses the generated reader, info object, and record, then reads the published data:

<!-- source: examples/presentation/demo/ViewMain.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Service;
import ecs.Tick;

/** Reads the projection without keeping ECS rows or engine objects in the world. */
final class ViewMain {
  public static function main(): Void {
    final scope = Check.accepted(ViewPlan.open_scope(new Service()));
    final world = Check.accepted(scope.create_world(ViewPlan.default_resource_init()));
    Check.ok(world.spawn_actor());
    final session = Check.accepted(ViewPlan.session(world));
    final reader = ViewPlan.new_render_reader();
    final info = ViewPlan.new_render_info();
    final record = ViewPlan.new_render_record();

    final clock = new LocalLoop(session);
    Check.ok(clock.frame(0.10));
    Check.ok(clock.frame(0.15));
    Check.ok(session.acquire_render(reader));
    Check.ok(reader.describe(info));
    Check.that(info.count == 1, "One actor is visible");
    for (index in 0...info.count) {
      Check.ok(reader.read(index, record));
      // Copy these values to presentation objects owned by the application.
      Check.that(record.x == 0.5, "Predicted position is available before verify");
      Check.that(record.elapsed == 0.25, "The match clock advanced once");
    }
    Check.ok(reader.release());
    Check.ok(session.close());
    Sys.println('ecs presentation example ok');
  }
}
```

In Heaps or another engine layer, copy the record's values to objects owned by that layer. The example has one actor; a multi-entity presentation also needs the application's identity mapping. Do not use a read index or physical WEID as a permanent scene-object key. Release the reader after copying the data and before dependent lifecycle operations.

### Choose the output for the job

| Output | Typical use | Consumer responsibility |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `state` | Current render or UI projection | Acquire, read, release; follow the chosen source and correction contract |
| `changes` | Incremental updates to an external view | Track its correction or invalidation semantics |
| `events` | Bounded sequence of occurrences | Track the sequence cursor and retained range |
| `trace` | Local execution diagnostics | Keep diagnostic consumption outside game rules |
| `effect` | Confirmed sound, notification, or external action | Apply successfully, then acknowledge its key |
| `immediate` / `confirmed` callback | Short synchronous application hook | Respect Session ownership and callback lifetime |

The effect outbox lets a consumer acknowledge successful delivery. It is not a transaction with your payment service, file system, or network. Persisted consumers still need their own idempotency and recovery rules. Irreversible side effects should not be performed directly by replayable system handlers.

## The application game loop

The library does not own the engine's frame callback or wall clock. This small [LocalLoop](examples/presentation/demo/LocalLoop.hx) adapts elapsed real time to the example's four ticks per second and limits work to two new ticks per visual frame:

<!-- source: examples/presentation/demo/LocalLoop.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Status;
import ecs.Tick;

/** Application-owned clock for the example's four simulation ticks per second. */
final class LocalLoop {
  final session: ViewPlanSession;
  var boundary = Tick.zero();
  var accumulated = 0.0;

  public function new(session: ViewPlanSession) {
    this.session = session;
  }

  /** Retains elapsed time and executes at most two ticks per presentation frame. */
  public function frame(elapsed: Float): Status {
    if (!Math.isFinite(elapsed) || elapsed < 0.0)
      return Status.InvalidArgument;
    accumulated += elapsed;
    var steps = 0;
    while (accumulated >= 0.25 && steps < 2) {
      final next = Check.accepted(boundary.next());
      final result = session.advance(next);
      if (result != Status.Ok)
        return result;
      boundary = next;
      accumulated -= 0.25;
      steps++;
    }
    return accumulated >= 0.25 ? Status.Pending : Status.Ok;
  }
}
```

`ViewMain` passes `0.10` and `0.15` seconds in two calls and observes exactly one simulation tick. The adapter retains excess time; a game chooses its own catch-up limit, pause policy, and presentation interpolation. This clock is deliberately local and has no network input scheduling.

For a fixed-rate network game, sample devices into input frames for explicit ticks, deliver network batches, advance toward the chosen predicted boundary, and verify only the available authoritative input. Read responsive state separately from confirmed effects. A pending replay must continue across engine frames instead of blocking the render loop in an unbounded retry loop.

For a local variable-step game, a Plan can use `Blueprint.variableLocal(maxTickDt)` and receive `advance(target, dt)`. The duration is saved for replay. VariableLocal is not the networked fixed-tick mode, and repeated rendering of a frame must not cause duplicate simulation steps.

## Input, prediction, and rollback

An input frame stores fixed-size data. `write_int32(offset, value)` writes an integer at a byte offset; the system reads it with `read_int32(place, offset)`. The first example uses offset `0` and input place `0`.

An input batch names a `Tick`; `add(place, seat_revision, revision, frame)` supplies one place's frame. In the initial session, seat revision is `0` and the first frame revision is `1`. A later frame revision can replace a place's data while that tick's input is incomplete. Seat revision identifies the place assignment and is a separate value from frame revision.

When every active place has supplied its frame, the tick's input is sealed **even before `verify`**. An exact repeat remains harmless; a newer revision or changed value returns `Stale`. After verification, submissions for that tick are stale as well.

### Missing input is an explicit policy

| Policy | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| `Wait` | Stop at unavailable required input |
| `Zero` | Predict zero input |
| `Hold` | Predict by holding the previous input |

Configure a place through `session.configure_input(place, seat_revision, active, policy)`. Prediction advances responsive simulation; it does not confirm missing input. A producer thread can push batches into `InputInbox`; Session mutation remains on its owner thread.

### Correct a tick already simulated

[RollbackPlan](examples/timeline/demo/RollbackPlan.hx) uses the first example's movement rule with `input_capacity: 2`. [RollbackMain](examples/timeline/demo/RollbackMain.hx) first simulates place zero's revision `1` while place one is missing, replaces it with revision `2`, then completes input and replays. It also checks rejection of another revision after the input is sealed. `Check` is the example's small [result helper](examples/demo/Check.hx), which throws on an unexpected refusal.

<!-- source: examples/timeline/demo/RollbackMain.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Service;
import ecs.Tick;
import ecs.Status;
import ecs.rollback.SessionState;

/** Replaces unverified input and checks that replay replaces the old effect. */
final class RollbackMain {
  public static function main(): Void {
    final scope = Check.accepted(RollbackPlan.open_scope(new Service()));
    final world = Check.accepted(scope.create_world(RollbackPlan.default_resource_init()));
    Check.ok(world.spawn_actor());
    final session = Check.accepted(RollbackPlan.session(world));
    final frame = RollbackPlan.input_frame();
    final batch = RollbackPlan.input_batch();
    final reader = RollbackPlan.new_effect_reader();
    final position = RollbackPlan.new_position_effect();
    final state = new SessionState();
    final zero = Tick.zero();
    final one = Check.accepted(zero.next());

    // Place 1 is still missing, so place 0 can receive revisions.
    // Revision 1 predicts x = (2 + 1) * 0.25 = 0.75.
    Check.ok(frame.write_int32(0, 1));
    Check.ok(batch.reset(zero));
    Check.ok(batch.add(
      0,
      0,
      1,
      frame
    ));
    Check.ok(session.submit(batch));
    Check.ok(session.advance(one));

    // Replace place 0 while this tick still has incomplete input.
    Check.ok(frame.write_int32(0, 3));
    Check.ok(batch.reset(zero));
    Check.ok(batch.add(
      0,
      0,
      2,
      frame
    ));
    Check.ok(session.submit(batch));
    // Complete input with the second place; this seals the tick's input.
    Check.ok(frame.write_int32(0, 0));
    Check.ok(batch.reset(zero));
    Check.ok(batch.add(
      1,
      0,
      1,
      frame
    ));
    Check.ok(session.submit(batch));

    // Even before verify, a newer revision of complete input is stale.
    Check.ok(frame.write_int32(0, 9));
    Check.ok(batch.reset(zero));
    Check.ok(batch.add(
      0,
      0,
      3,
      frame
    ));
    Check.that(session.submit(batch) == Status.Stale, "Complete input rejects revision changes");
    Check.ok(session.state(state));

    Check.that(state.dirty.equals(zero), "Corrected input marks tick zero dirty");
    Check.ok(session.advance(one));
    Check.ok(session.verify(one));
    Check.ok(session.peekEffects(reader));
    Check.ok(reader.read_position(position));
    Check.that(position.x == 1.25, "Replay starts from the saved state");
    Check.ok(session.ackEffectsThrough(reader.key));
    Check.ok(session.close());
    Sys.println('ecs rollback example ok');
  }
}
```

The final position is `1.25`, rather than the old `0.75` plus another movement. The replay restores a saved boundary and runs the same game rule with the replacement input. The original unverified effect is replaced as part of that replay.

`SessionState` exposes predicted and verified boundaries, the first dirty tick, and progress. `CatchingUp` means replay is continuing in bounded portions. `Stalled` means the requested boundary exceeds the current working limit. `Status.Pending` is a reason to inspect progress and continue when its condition permits; it is not a reason to spin indefinitely.

The game still owns packet transport, player authority, input delay policy, random number state, and any external algorithm that affects replay. Confirming input does not prove that a remote player is trustworthy.

## Snapshots and state transfer

A snapshot represents `Prepared(t)`: the boundary after the tick prologue and before its first Process. It contains the authoritative state needed to resume execution. Allocate the Plan's snapshot buffer once, then export through Session.

[SnapshotMain](examples/timeline/demo/SnapshotMain.hx) runs two ticks, exports the retained boundary at tick one, loads it into a new Service, and continues from the saved position. It compares the canonical hash after advancing beyond the loaded boundary:

<!-- source: examples/timeline/demo/SnapshotMain.hx -->
```haxe
package demo;

import ecs.Service;
import ecs.Tick;
import ecs.diagnostics.Hash64;

/** Loads a retained checkpoint in a separate Service and continues the timeline. */
final class SnapshotMain {
  public static function main(): Void {
    final source_scope = Check.accepted(GamePlan.open_scope(new Service()));
    final world = Check.accepted(source_scope.create_world(GamePlan.default_resource_init()));
    Check.ok(world.spawn_actor());
    final source = Check.accepted(GamePlan.session(world));
    final frame = GamePlan.input_frame();
    final batch = GamePlan.input_batch();
    final one = Check.accepted(Tick.at(0, 1));
    final two = Check.accepted(one.next());
    final reader = GamePlan.new_effect_reader();
    final position = GamePlan.new_position_effect();

    for (number in 0...2) {
      Check.ok(frame.write_int32(0, number == 0 ? 3 : 1));
      Check.ok(batch.reset(Check.accepted(Tick.at(0, number))));
      Check.ok(batch.add(
        0,
        0,
        1,
        frame
      ));
      final target = Check.accepted(Tick.at(0, number + 1));
      Check.ok(source.submit(batch));
      Check.ok(source.advance(target));
      Check.ok(source.verify(target));
      Check.ok(source.peekEffects(reader));
      Check.ok(source.ackEffectsThrough(reader.key));
    }

    final bytes = GamePlan.snapshot_buffer();
    Check.ok(source.write_snapshot(one, bytes));
    final target_scope = Check.accepted(GamePlan.open_scope(new Service()));
    final loaded_world = Check.accepted(target_scope.load_world(bytes));
    final loaded = Check.accepted(GamePlan.session(loaded_world));

    final expected = new Hash64();
    final actual = new Hash64();
    Check.ok(source.hash(one, expected));

    Check.ok(frame.write_int32(0, 1));
    Check.ok(batch.reset(one));
    Check.ok(batch.add(
      0,
      0,
      1,
      frame
    ));
    Check.ok(loaded.submit(batch));
    Check.ok(loaded.advance(two));
    // A retained hash is readable only for ticks strictly before Predicted.
    Check.ok(loaded.hash(one, actual));
    Check.that(expected.equals(actual), "Loaded checkpoint has the same canonical hash");
    Check.ok(loaded.verify(two));
    Check.ok(loaded.peekEffects(reader));
    Check.ok(reader.read_position(position));
    Check.that(position.x == 2.0, "Loaded world continues from x = 1.25");
    Check.ok(loaded.ackEffectsThrough(reader.key));
    Check.ok(loaded.close());
    Check.ok(source.close());
    Sys.println('ecs snapshot example ok');
  }
}
```

Loading checks the snapshot envelope and Plan identity before creating the world. Matching class names alone do not establish compatibility. `write_snapshot` and `hash` read retained, restored ticks strictly earlier than `Predicted`; the current boundary is not yet a historical entry. A hash equality is a state-comparison check, not a cryptographic authentication protocol.

For an existing Session, `correct_snapshot(bytes)` and `revise_snapshot(bytes, revision)` apply corrections under their timeline contracts. `write_delta(base, target, delta)` writes a difference; `correct_deltas` and `revise_deltas` accept a chain. A delta needs its expected base and the same Plan compatibility as a full snapshot.

Use these facilities for checkpoint transfer, reconnecting to a match, or bounded replay. A snapshot is not a promised permanent save format across schema changes and future library versions. Long-term saves, migration, compression, packet framing, and authority validation belong to the application.

## Memory and identifiers

### Plan capacity instead of relying on growth

An archetype placement stores columns in an aligned layout. Its rows are partitioned as `[active][free/default][sleepy]`. Moving a row updates all its columns and the slot-to-row mapping; a physical row number is therefore not a stable identity.

For a placement `a` and groups `g`, capacity follows:

```text
base[a]     = direct[a] + sum(guaranteed[g] * need[g, a])
overflow[a] = max((maximum[g] - guaranteed[g]) * need[g, a])
capacity[a] = base[a] + overflow[a]
```

Every group gets its guarantee. Any one group may reach its maximum; simultaneous excesses compete for one shared overflow reserve. `need` includes the nodes required by permitted prefabs. Without groups, overflow is zero.

Plan entity storage together with the rollback window, input places, observations, effects, and snapshots. Reducing live entity count alone does not remove the memory reserved for history and buffers. `Plan.memory_report` and `session.memory_report()` describe accounted storage; they do not measure the process's total RSS, GC metadata, stack, or native code.

### Use the right identifier at each boundary

| Identifier | Purpose and lifetime |
| --- | --- |
| Generated Plan IDs | Typed names for definitions in one Plan and one category |
| `WEID` | Physical generational address, confined to handlers of its World |
| `EEID` | Opaque logical entity identity within one Service; survives physical Scope replacement |
| `Tick` | Timeline coordinate with `era` and `number`; advance with its API |

WEID32 uses `5 + 15 + 12` bits for home Area, node slot, and generation. WEID64 uses `5 + 15 + 44` bits in two Int32 columns. The larger profile extends generation capacity; it does not make an address permanent. WEID32 provides an explicit `session.rebase(successor_scope)` transition at an admissible clean boundary.

EEID uses 53 exact bits, with a public codec for Lua Number interoperability. A Service can issue at most 3,840 logical world identities over its lifetime; the limit is not a simultaneous-world count. Neither WEID nor EEID is an eternal database key. Keep packing, generation changes, and stale-reference checks inside the library's API.

### Parallel execution

Execution is sequential with zero workers. An explicit `ExecutionPoolConfig` enables workers where declared rights prove independence. The owner merges their private results in Plan order. Dependencies, resource conflicts, and publication boundaries still impose sequencing, so more workers do not guarantee a speedup.

In an `ecs_parallel` build, locks created with each store protect the shared counters and maps of deferred writes. Their cold cost and publication boundaries are described in the [contributor guide](CONTRIBUTING.md#данные-и-публикация).

Service, Scope, World, and Session have an owning thread. The documented exceptions are one InputInbox producer and reader acquisition from another thread; an acquired reader then belongs to that thread until release. Atomic operations are implemented for HashLink and the Haxe interpreter. Other threaded targets, including cpp, fail compilation with `ECS-ATOMIC-001` until an atomic backend is provided.

Create a pool with `HashLinkExecutionPool.create(config, receipt)`, reusing an `ExecutionPoolCreateReceipt`. Failures distinguish `InvalidArgument`, `Unsupported`, `Exhausted`, and `Faulted`; the receipt preserves the status, creation stage, worker index, and message.

A worker timeout permanently faults the pool and its active sessions. Waiting calls return `Faulted` with `ExecutionRunReceipt.recoverable == false`. The core retains state that a worker may still access and refuses recovery or reuse, even after the worker returns. `session.execution_receipt(output)` remains available. The application's `ExecutionFatalHandler` decides whether to terminate the process; the core never calls `Sys.exit`. A completed worker failure still supports the existing Prepared restoration and `recover_execution` path. External runners use contract version 4 and must mark failures with outstanding worker access as unrecoverable.

## Trade-offs

**The schema is static.** Adding an unknown component type at runtime is outside this model. Changing the schema requires compilation, and large plans add macro and generated-code build time.

**Capacity is a design input.** Plan entity limits, rollback history, and output buffers before running a world. More retained state consumes more memory; `MemoryReport` does not include the process's entire RSS, GC metadata, stack, or code.

**Rollback requires application discipline.** Physics, navigation, randomness, networking, persistence, and irreversible side effects must follow the game's replay contract. The library supplies the timeline and state machinery, not a complete networking or physics engine.

**Parallelism is conditional.** Dependencies and publication boundaries still impose order. Direct pair iteration can be quadratic; large collision sets need a suitable spatial algorithm supplied by the application.

**Numerical and platform guarantees need verification.** A deterministic system order alone does not make arbitrary floating-point code identical across devices. Choose an appropriate numerical profile and validate the full simulation on each shipping target.

For a small game without replay requirements, an object model or a more dynamic ECS may require less setup. Browser JavaScript, arbitrary objects in component columns, unlimited automatic growth, and runtime schema registration are outside this library's intended use.

## API guide and maintenance

With your application's HXML selected in a Haxe IDE, `Blueprint` forms provide completion, signature help, hover documentation with small examples, and navigation to their declarations in [Blueprint.hx](src/ecs/Blueprint.hx). This includes nested calls and unfinished declarations. `Expr` in these signatures means an expression in the DSL; you do not construct Haxe AST objects. The build macros still validate the complete declaration. These hints cover `Blueprint` calls; they do not describe the generated members of `access` inside handlers.

The bundled [ECS guide](agents/skills/ecs/SKILL.md) describes the API vocabulary, integration, systems, prefabs, identifiers, and memory. It ships with the library and does not require the research repository or its agent memory.

Run the native `memory` command from the skill directory, select relevant entries in the complete index, and then read their bodies:

```sh
(cd agents/skills/ecs && memory index --prefix TPL)
(cd agents/skills/ecs && memory index --prefix EXP)
```

The Markdown data file contains indexed records, not an instruction to load the entire history. Public API changes must update the guide and affected examples together. Changes are developed and validated in this repository.

Ruby is used by documentation and development tools; it is not a game runtime dependency. The [test registry](test/registry.tsv) records each check's purpose, expected result, estimated duration, wait interval, timeout, and latest observation. See [validation](VALIDATION.md) and [packaging](PACKAGING.md) for the exact local checks.

## Validation and performance

The implementation comes from research iteration 51. Its full set of **432 correctness checks passed** on macOS Apple Silicon, including API diagnostics, replay, state transfer, threading, and memory checks. Independent integration examples passed with both WEID profiles.

The recorded environment is Apple M3 Max, macOS 26.5.2 arm64, Apple clang 21, Haxe development `ccf17df`, and HashLink master `864721a` (2.0.0). Pin the complete toolchain when reproducing results. Heaps is not required by the core.

An internal comparison reduced CPU time for canonical copying of 65,536 Float64 values with 25% zeros by a factor of **4.8697**, with a nominal 95% interval of **[4.8650, 4.8745]**, on that machine. All 192 comparison executions preserved the expected values with zero measured allocation increase. Ordinary snapshot capture and restore did not show an established speedup. This result describes that operation and workload; it is neither an overall ECS speed claim nor a comparison against another library.

Nintendo Switch, PS4, PS5, Xbox Series S/X, Windows, Linux, iOS, and Android are intended HL/C targets. Their SDK integration, execution, and performance have not been validated here. See [validation details](VALIDATION.md) for the precise evidence and limits.

